/*
 * L3, assets (threat model `assets`): an lvalue only its declared writers write, and, when the
 * threat model lists readers, only they read. It generalizes the auth state of Layer 4 to any
 * integrity or confidentiality asset. The targets are listed by name: every function but the
 * declared ones (scripts/security_props.py computes the set; never \inter or \in_file).
 *   writers  context \writing: before each write, the written location is separated from the asset
 *   readers  context \reading: before each read, the read location is separated from the asset
 * Clauses, joined by &&:
 *   global  the asset is a global lvalue (`config`, `session.mode`): program-wide
 *   formal  the asset hangs off a parameter (`c->mode`): one clause per parameter name the object
 *           is passed as (the lvalue's root and the threat model's `params`), each checked in the
 *           functions that have a parameter of that name (\tguard: \true elsewhere). A helper that
 *           takes the object under another name escapes the check: list that name.
 * A call placed in an initializer gets no \writing instance from the callee's assigns (MetAcsl
 * 0.12): security_props.py writes `init_write` as a `check` into the work tree there, `region`
 * being what the callee assigns with the call's arguments in place of its parameters.
 */
//# writers
/*@ meta \prop, \name(${name}), \targets(${targets}), \context(\writing),
      ${predicate};
*/
//# readers
/*@ meta \prop, \name(${name}), \targets(${targets}), \context(\reading),
      ${predicate};
*/
//# writers_global
\separated(\written, &${lvalue})
//# writers_formal
\tguard(\separated(\written, &${lvalue}))
//# readers_global
\separated(\read, &${lvalue})
//# readers_formal
\tguard(\separated(\read, &${lvalue}))
//# init_write
\separated(${region}, &${lvalue})
